{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Rejoice, Zebulun, etc.</b> Even though in the matters of this world, in general, there is no cause for rejoicing in them, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 2:2) ״וּלְשִׂמְחָה מַה זֹּה עֹשָׂה״, the prophet said that Zebulun is in joy, even in his going forth from the house of study to engage in commerce, and the reason is in order that Issachar may be established in his tent, as he said: ״וְיִשָּׂשכָר בְּאֹהָלֶיךָ״." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that since a person does not know at the time of his departure for commerce whether he will succeed or not until he returns successfully, therefore he said to Zebulun: ״rejoice״ from the time of your departure, for you will certainly succeed. And the reason is that since Issachar is in your tents — you are the agent of a mitzvah to sustain those who study Torah — and he called it the tent of Issachar with reference to Zebulun, for he is the one who builds his tent." "<b>Or</b> it may mean that ״in your tents״ is an appellation for Issachar, and it used the language of tent for two reasons: first, because one does not make a permanent dwelling in this world but only a temporary sojourn, and their permanent dwelling is the World to Come. Second, it is an allusion to that which shelters over them — namely, the Shechinah, which is called a tent. Even though they engage in commerce, concerning them it is said (Psalms 85:14) ״צֶדֶק לְפָנָיו יְהַלֵּךְ״, etc."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:22:17.198615256Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}